, 4, 5, 1, 2, 3, 5, 6, 7, 0, 1, 5, 0, 5, 4, 1, 2, 6, 1, 6, 5, 2, 3, 7, 2, 7, 6, 3, 0, 4, 3, 4, 7]}, "m": "metal"}, {"tree": [-30.0, 15.0, 10, 3.2]}, {"tree": [10.0, -5.0, 10, 3.2]}, {"box": [-33.0, 20.0, 0, 6, 6, 2.2], "m": "sage"}, {"box": [6.0, -8.0, 0, 6, 6, 2.2], "m": "sage"}], "notes": "Herzog & de Meuron's de Young (2005) in Golden Gate Park, from published descriptions and dimensions and the ground file's footprint (a long block about 150 x 84 m turned some 43 degrees off north, its long side on the Music Concourse to the south-east): three long, slightly splayed bars clad in perforated copper, now weathered dark brown about 13.5 m high, cut by glazed courts with eucalyptus and ferns; the great canopy cantilevered over the entrance court on the concourse side; the 44 m Hamon Tower at the north-east end, a slab that twists from the building's line to the city grid as it rises, its top floor glazed for the observation deck. Guessed: the exact bar widths and splay, court positions, tower plan dimensions. No photographs were available. Refined 26 Sep 2026: added the cantilevered copper-clad entrance canopy over the north forecourt (about 40 x 20 m, floating clear of the ground) \u2014 the building's single most photographed feature, missing from the first model. Refined 26 Sep 2026 (still no photographs reachable, Wikimedia blocked): added the cantilevered entrance canopy at the building's west corner (about 24 x 13 m, floating clear of the wall) \u2014 its single most photographed feature, missing from the first model, though sited only where the model's crowded footprint left it room, not at its true position; two of the smaller wall segments changed from solid copper to the perforated-screen material so the building reads as pierced panels rather than one solid mass, closer to the real weathered-copper skin; more eucalyptus and low fern planting in the glass courts. Still guessed: the exact bar widths and splay, court positions, tower plan dimensions, and the canopy's true siting and shape."}